  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAt the time of writing, the heavy rain shield attached to a\npotent coastal low pressure system has lifted northward. A\ncombination of drizzle and light rain remains noted within our\nforecast area this afternoon, embedded within blanketed overcast\nskies. Should see the coverage of showers increase once more\nover the next few hours, as seen upstream in central Kentucky\nalready underway. So far today, local reporting weather stations\nmaps show a stripe of 1 to 1.5\" of rain having already fallen\nacross portions of southeast Ohio into northeast West Virginia.\nLesser amounts have been observed for the southern half of the\nforecast area, strongly due to dry slotting that began around\ndaybreak this morning.\n\nStarting this evening, thermal profiles along the foothills and\nhigher terrain will begin to transform into a more wintry \nregime as colder air is drawn into the area on the backside of \nthis powerful disturbance. Should begin to see a transition to a\nwintry mix in southern Kentucky and Virginia just before\nmidnight, then stretching up the spine of the Appalachians\novernight. Heading into Friday, ongoing showers will fall\nprimarily as rain in the lowlands, while a rain/snow mixture\ncontinues along the foothills, and as all snow for elevations \nhigher than 2,000ft AGL. Precipitation coverage should gradually\npeter out from west to east late in the day on Friday as the\ncoastal system travels up the New England coast, while post-\nfrontal northwesterly flow will encourage ongoing snow along our\nmountain zones. Light snow amounts of a few tenths of an inch is\nstill on track with this event, with pockets of locally higher\namounts on the ridges.\n\nAdditionally, this expansive storm has contributed to a large\ncoverage of strong wind gusts over the eastern half of the\ncountry in response to a tightened pressure gradient. As of the\ntime of writing, the strongest recorded gust in our forecast\narea has been 74mph at Snowshoe, firmly placed in the ongoing\nHigh Wind Warning. While overall gusts have come down some since\nthis morning, will maintain ongoing Warning and Advisory wind\nheadlines into the 7 PM expiration. Widespread gusty winds\nreturn on Friday to the region, with another window of advisory\nlevel winds along our mountain zones. In collaboration with\nneighboring offices, opted to hold off on additional headlines\nuntil the current issuance comes to an end to help avoid\nconfusion. Gradient winds are progged to ease slightly over the\nweekend once the coastal low lessens its influence over the\nCentral Appalachians.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nBy Saturday morning, mountain snow showers will be wrapping up\nas moisture in the dendritic growth zone becomes depleted. A\nwindow of dry weather, albeit rather fleeting, will return to\nthe Central Appalachians then on Saturday into parts of Sunday.\nTemperatures slightly moderate to their climatological norms on\nSunday ahead of the next system sliding down from the upper\nMidwest. Precipitation, predominantly in the form of rain, will\npress from west to east beginning Sunday afternoon, then once \nagain wrapping up as mountain snow showers for the start of next\nweek.",
